      <p class="text-gray-900 font-semibold mb-2">
        I learned to verify, not assume.
      </p>
      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
        Clicking "accept" isn't the same as getting the job, so the bot
        checks for the confirmation before it tells her she's booked. A bot
        that assumes it worked is worse than no bot.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div
      class="bg-white rounded-xl p-5 border border-gray-200 shadow-sm hover:shadow-md transition-shadow"
    >
      <p class="text-gray-900 font-semibold mb-2">
        I learned to treat failures differently depending on what they are.
      </p>
      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
        A network blip should retry; a changed login page should stop and
        tell me. Sorting errors into recoverable versus terminal, instead of
        one catch-all, is what made it trustworthy.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div
      class="bg-white rounded-xl p-5 border border-gray-200 shadow-sm hover:shadow-md transition-shadow"
    >
      <p class="text-gray-900 font-semibold mb-2">
        I learned that most of the work is turning a person's needs into
        rules.
      </p>
      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
        Her real what-ifs (only same-day jobs, a heads-up before it accepts)
        had to become actual logic. Requirements live in someone's head;
        getting them into code correctly is the job.
      </p>
